{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nWhen a store write cannot acquire the dual-window write lock inside its budget, the write is\nDISCARDED — no retry, no spool. `acquire()` computes `total_wait = timeout * attempts` and feeds\n**one** deadline (`_store/lock.py:695-698`), so `attempts` is a silent multiplier, not a retry\nloop. Both legs non-blocking-poll with jitter (50ms fcntl `lock.py:215`, 100ms mkdir\n`lock.py:628`), so there is no fairness. `LockTimeout` is raised and the caller's write is gone.\n\nRe-measured 2026-08-11 against an APFS clone of the live 737M tracker (full numbers in the AC#1\ncomment on this ticket):\n\n* **The report's 6-writer storm no longer loses writes** — 6/6, 16/16 and 48/48 concurrent\n  `rebar comment`s all succeed (max 14.9s at 48). 7084's hold-duration fixes were real.\n* **Loss is still reachable and terminal, now driven by HOLDER DURATION.** A 70s holder plus 3\n  comments → 3/3 exit 1 with `flock: could not acquire lock after 60s`, and 0 of the 3 markers\n  present in the store afterwards. A 45s holder plus one `rebar claim` → fails at exactly\n  **30.30s** (`attempts=1`), dying while comments beside it survive. A 40s holder left 6 comments\n  succeeding at **59.5s of a 60s budget** — a sub-second margin.\n\nField-confirmed: the live Jira DC CI run recorded on this ticket spent the 30s budget on an\nordinary runner, and the lost write broke the reconciler's idempotency invariant.\n\n## Approach\n\n**Bounded retry-with-backoff, not fair queueing.** The measurement selects the remedy: when a\nsingle holder outlives the whole budget, *every* waiter fails regardless of service order, so FIFO\nfairness buys nothing against the failure that actually occurs. Extending the budget as bounded\nre-attempts is what converts loss into latency, with strictly less machinery than a queue.\n\nRetry is safe here **without** the phantom-write semantics that got R6 (spool-and-adopt) declined\non 7084: `LockTimeout` is raised by `acquire()` *before* anything is staged or committed, so a\nre-attempt cannot double-write. Nothing becomes visible-but-uncommitted.\n\n1. `acquire()` and its `write_lock()` wrapper gain **`retries: int = 0`**. The default of **0\n   reproduces today's behaviour exactly**, so every caller not listed as opted-in below is\n   unchanged with no edit. On a pass whose budget expires, the loop sleeps a jittered backoff and\n   re-attempts the whole acquire, up to `retries` extra passes.\n2. Opted-in sites pass `retries=_write_path_retries()`, a helper reading the env override\n   **`REBAR_LOCK_RETRIES`** (integer, **default 2**, clamped to `[0, 10]`). `REBAR_LOCK_RETRIES=0`\n   restores today's exact fail-fast for CI/ops. Opted in, the write path's ceiling becomes ~180s\n   (3 × 60s), riding out the measured 70s holder instead of discarding the write.\n3. The terminating `LockTimeout` names the **cumulative** wait actually spent plus the holder, so\n   the message cannot understate how long the caller waited; each exhausted pass logs a warning\n   naming the holder, so starvation is visible even where callers swallow the exception.\n4. `txn._acquire_write_lock` (`txn.py:84-86`) currently catches `LockTimeout` and re-raises a\n   generic `CommandError(\"Error: could not acquire lock\")`, discarding both the wait and the\n   holder. It is changed to propagate `str(exc)`, so AC#4's honest message survives on the claim\n   and transition path — the verb the measurement shows failing first.\n\n## Scope\n\n**Edited — opting in to retries:**\n- `src/rebar/_store/lock.py` — `retries` parameter (default 0), the retry loop, the\n  `REBAR_LOCK_RETRIES` helper, cumulative-wait `LockTimeout` message, per-pass warning.\n- `src/rebar/_store/event_append.py` — the canonical write path; the `write_lock()` calls at\n  **382, 420 and 532** (`stage_and_commit`, `delete_events`) opt in. This is the mechanism behind\n  `rebar comment` and is what the ~180s ceiling claim is about.\n- `src/rebar/_commands/txn.py` — `_acquire_write_lock` (**84**) opts in, and propagates the\n  `LockTimeout` message into `CommandError` instead of the generic string.\n- `src/rebar/_store/push.py` — **664** (the locked ticket commit-and-push entrypoint) is a genuine\n  write and opts in. **375** is the advisory push-retry merge and stays fail-fast.\n- `tests/unit/test_lock_retry_zebrafish.py` (new) — the tests below.\n- `docs/concurrency.md` — the retry ceiling, the opted-in site list, and `REBAR_LOCK_RETRIES`.\n\n**Deliberately NOT edited — every remaining `acquire()`/`write_lock()` caller keeps fail-fast via\nthe `retries=0` default:**\n- `compact_txn.py:340`, `compact_rebuild.py:126` (`attempts=2`) — compaction MUST keep standing\n  aside when the lock is busy; retries here would undo 7084's R3 and re-create the 48s holder that\n  caused this bug.\n- `fsck_repair.py:241` (`attempts=2`), `s3_doctor.py:199` (`attempts=1`) — operator-driven repair;\n  failing fast so the operator sees contention is the correct posture.\n- `inbound_translate.py:283` (`attempts=1`) — reconciler pass, already retried at its own\n  advisory-lock layer.\n- `sync.py:255`, `push.py:375`, `ensures.py:158` — best-effort advisory sweeps; lengthening them\n  would delay unrelated commands.\n\n## Testing\n\n- **Happy path:** an uncontended `acquire(retries=2)` takes the lock on the first pass and never\n  sleeps a backoff — the retry loop adds no cost absent contention.\n- **The reproduction (RED→GREEN):** a real subprocess holder held past a single budget, with a\n  waiter whose per-pass budget is shorter than the hold. With `retries=0` the waiter raises\n  `LockTimeout` (today's loss); with `retries=2` it acquires on a later pass and its write **is**\n  present. Scaled to seconds (≈2s budget vs ≈3s hold) rather than reproducing the literal 70s case.\n- **Failure/edge path:** a holder outliving *all* passes still raises `LockTimeout` — the write\n  fails LOUDLY rather than hanging — and the message reports the **cumulative** wait, not one\n  pass's budget.\n- **The claim case:** a `claim`-shaped acquire behind a holder outliving one 30s-equivalent budget\n  succeeds on a later pass; and its `CommandError` text carries the cumulative wait and holder\n  rather than the generic `Error: could not acquire lock`.\n- **Env override:** `REBAR_LOCK_RETRIES=0` restores single-budget fail-fast at an opted-in site,\n  and an out-of-range value clamps rather than raising.\n- **Edge:** `retries=0` reproduces today's exact single-budget behaviour, proving the\n  deliberately-unedited callers above are unchanged.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] Re-measure the contention harness against post-R1/R3/R5 main and record whether writes are\n      still lost — **done**, numbers in the AC#1 comment on this ticket: the 6-writer storm no\n      longer loses writes, but a holder outliving the budget still loses 3/3 writes and `claim`\n      still dies at 30.3s.\n- [ ] The failure-shape decision is recorded in this description with the evidence selecting it —\n      bounded retry-with-backoff over fair queueing — verified by the Approach section stating why\n      FIFO does not address a single over-budget holder.\n- [ ] A write whose first acquire budget expires is **re-attempted**, not discarded, verified by\n      `pytest tests/unit/test_lock_retry_zebrafish.py` where the reproduction case fails with\n      `retries=0` and passes with `retries=2`.\n- [ ] A write that exhausts every pass still fails LOUDLY with a `LockTimeout` whose message\n      reports the **cumulative** wait and the holder, verified by asserting the message text in\n      the exhaustion test.\n- [ ] `claim`'s `attempts=1` budget is corrected and its error is no longer generic, verified by a\n      test that a claim-shaped acquire behind an over-budget holder succeeds on a later pass, and\n      that the raised `CommandError` text contains the cumulative wait and holder.\n- [ ] Every `acquire()`/`write_lock()` caller is classified: opted-in sites are exactly\n      `event_append.py` (382/420/532), `txn.py` (84) and `push.py` (664); all others retain\n      fail-fast through the `retries=0` default. Verified by a test asserting the default is 0,\n      plus `grep` showing no other call site passes `retries`.\n- [ ] `REBAR_LOCK_RETRIES` bounds the ceiling, verified by a test that `REBAR_LOCK_RETRIES=0`\n      restores single-budget timeout behaviour at an opted-in site and that out-of-range values\n      clamp to `[0, 10]`.\n- [ ] `docs/concurrency.md` documents the retry ceiling, the opted-in site list and\n      `REBAR_LOCK_RETRIES` with its default, verified by `grep REBAR_LOCK_RETRIES docs/concurrency.md`.\n- [ ] `make lint`, `make typecheck`, the new test file and the existing\n      `tests/integration/test_store_concurrency.py` all pass, and `src/rebar/_store/lock.py`\n      stays under the 800-line module cap."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786514058881831001,"uuid":"9431bf38-cf1e-4fc9-b507-6e6d8a34224e"}